1. **An electric power control method** for controlling power supplied from an AC power source to a load by a switching device, the method comprising:

   generating a control signal corresponding to a desired power amount to be supplied to the load;

   comparing the control signal with a carrier wave to determine switching timings of the switching device within an operation period including a plurality of half cycles of the AC power source;

   extending the operation period from a reference operation period to an extended operation period including a greater number of half cycles than the reference operation period;

   driving the switching device during the extended operation period in accordance with a result of the comparison between the control signal and the carrier wave; and

   reducing switching of the switching device in a first half cycle and a last half cycle of the extended operation period relative to switching performed in one or more intermediate half cycles of the extended operation period,

   wherein the reducing of switching suppresses a change in power supplied to the load at a beginning and an end of the extended operation period while maintaining, over the extended operation period, an average supplied power corresponding to the desired power amount.

1. A method for controlling an inverter, the method comprising:

measuring a current output from the inverter;

calculating, based at least on the measured current, a duty command for controlling switching elements of the inverter;

generating a carrier wave having an extended carrier-wave operation period that is longer than a normal carrier-wave period;

adjusting a slope of the carrier wave within the extended carrier-wave operation period such that a change rate of the carrier wave is reduced at a beginning portion and an end portion of the extended carrier-wave operation period; and

controlling switching operations of the switching elements by comparing the duty command with the carrier wave having the adjusted slope,

wherein the adjusted slope reduces a number of switching operations of the switching elements at the beginning portion and the end portion of the extended carrier-wave operation period.

1. An electric power control method for controlling switching elements of a power converter, the electric power control method comprising:

measuring a current flowing in the power converter;

calculating, based on the measured current, a command value for controlling an output of the power converter;

determining, based on the command value, whether an operation period of the switching elements is to be lengthened to a period longer than one carrier cycle; and

controlling the switching elements in accordance with the command value,

wherein, when it is determined that the operation period is to be lengthened, the switching elements are controlled so that a switching state corresponding to the command value is maintained over a plurality of carrier cycles.

1. A method comprising:  

generating, by a controller of a pulse-width modulated power converter, a modulation signal corresponding to a desired converter output;  

generating a carrier wave for comparison with the modulation signal over a control interval comprising a first half-cycle, an intermediate period, and a last half-cycle;  

suppressing switching of at least one power switching device of the power converter during the first half-cycle and during the last half-cycle;  

during the intermediate period, changing a slope of the carrier wave relative to a slope used outside the intermediate period;  

comparing the modulation signal with the carrier wave having the changed slope during the intermediate period to produce pulse-width modulation control signals; and  

applying the pulse-width modulation control signals to the at least one power switching device during the intermediate period to control power conversion by the power converter.
